<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP is permitted, deliberately and reluctantly.

  A podcast client does not choose its URLs. Feeds hand us enclosure URLs written years ago, and a
  large share of them point at a cleartext measurement redirector — dts.podtrac.com is the one that
  prompted this file — which bounces through one or two more hops before reaching the publisher CDN.
  Since targetSdk 28 Android blocks cleartext outright, so such a show subscribes, refreshes and
  lists its episodes perfectly, then fails with a bare network error the moment it is played. There
  is no allowlist that would fix it: the hosts are whatever the world's feeds name, and the hops in
  between are not knowable until the redirect arrives.

  What keeps this from being a blanket downgrade is HttpsUpgradeInterceptor, which rewrites every
  outgoing http:// GET to https:// and only lets it fall back to cleartext when TLS genuinely is not
  on offer. In practice the permission granted here is exercised by the leftovers, not by the bulk
  of the traffic.

  Trust anchors are left at the platform default (system CAs only, no user-installed certificates),
  which is what targetSdk 24+ gives us and what we want: nothing here should make interception
  easier than it already is.
-->
<network-security-config xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!-- Suppressed rather than baselined: the header above is the reason, and it belongs here. -->
    <base-config
        cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"
        tools:ignore="InsecureBaseConfiguration" />

    <!--
      The hosts the app picks for itself, as opposed to the ones feeds pick for it, are HTTPS-only:
      the iTunes search API, and YouTube for extraction, audio and thumbnails. Every request the app
      makes to them is already written as https://, so a cleartext request to one of these could
      only come from a redirect or a downgrade the app never asked for. The platform refuses it
      here, before HttpsUpgradeInterceptor or anything else gets a say.
    -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">itunes.apple.com</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">youtube.com</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">youtu.be</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">googlevideo.com</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">ytimg.com</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
